Human: I want to create bold text in html, how can I achieve that? It’s not a good idea to create this effect manually. It’s better to use a CSS code in the <style> tag to accomplish this. The code looks like this:

<style>

body {

font-family: serif;

font-size: 18px;

line-height: 21px;

color: #FFF;

font-weight: bold;

}

.boldText {

font-weight: bold;

}

</style>

<div class="boldText">Here is bold text</div>

You could also use tags in HTML like this:

<b>Here is bold text</b>

But this is slightly less flexible and will only work if you know the exact HTML code to use for your situation.

Human: What does the b stand for in the html tag?

response:
In HTML, `b` is the tag for creating bold text.  You can also use the `strong` tag instead to create basically the same effect.  So to create bold text, you could do something like this:

<style>

.boldText {

font-weight: bold;

}

</style>

<p class="boldText">Here is bold text</p>

The `<b>` tag is used to create “bold” text.   A tag like `<strong>`, on the other hand, would normally be used to indicate that the content of the paragraph should be considered stronger than normal.   Also note that `<br />` is used here to create a line break, instead of `<p>` so that the paragraph doesn’t have any surrounding text.  This may or may not be how you will want your HTML tags to appear, depending on your particular requirements, but you can see that both of these will apply exactly the same effect to bold text.
